  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA large upper-level ridge will build east across much of the \nCentral and Eastern CONUS by the end of the weekend into mid-\nnext week. At the surface, a warm front will lift northeast \nthrough the area late Sunday into Monday, ushering in above-\naverage temperatures across the region. Highs in the mid to \nupper 80s on Sunday will increase further into the upper 80s to \nlower 90s by Monday, and perhaps reaching the mid-90s in some \nspots by Tuesday or Wednesday with the ridge axis overhead, \nyielding heat indices in the upper 90s to near 100 at times. All\nthis said, it is still not a slam-dunk heat event given \nincreasing chances for showers, storms, and residual cloud cover\nduring this same time period. Will continue to watch trends \nover the next couple of days. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAn unsettled weather pattern will develop across the Great Lakes\nby the end of the weekend into mid-next week as several mid-\nlevel shortwaves track east along the upper-level ridge axis,\nproviding increased flow needed to support organized convection.\n\nThe first period of concern will be Sunday night into Monday\nbehind the warm front as guidance favors the development of a \ncomplex of showers and storms generally moving southeast through\nthe Central and Eastern Great Lakes. This appears to be a \nglancing blow at this time, mainly across far Northeast Ohio or\nNorthwest Pennsylvania. Precipitation chances have generally \ntrended lower for Tuesday with the ridge axis overhead with \nchances increasing again by Tuesday night through Wednesday as a\nstrong upper-level trough swings east through the Upper Great \nLakes. In addition, anomalously-high PWATs in excess of 1.80 \ninches could lead to torrential rainfall and isolated flash \nflooding where storms persist. Still quite a bit of uncertainty \nwith regards to shower and thunderstorm timing and coverage, \nthough given the favorable environmental shear in place, the \nchance for strong to severe storms remains possible. Will\ncontinue to monitor trends.",
